{\cf0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/overview-of-scaling-vertical-and-horizontal-scaling/ }}{\fldrslt{https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/overview-of-scaling-vertical-and-horizontal-scaling/\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs32\par
\cf0\fs24 client who sends requests to the server and then the client receives a response from the server accordingly but when the number of users/clients increases, the load on the server increases  enormously which makes it difficult for the server to perform efficiently and hence becomes slow...to make the server scalable in a way such that the server capacity increases according to the increasing traffic without any sort of failure.\par
\par
\cf1 Scaling\par
\cf0 a process to expand the existing configuration (servers/computers) to handle a large number of user requests or to manage the amount of load on the server. This process is called scalability.\par
This can be done either by increasing the current system configuration (increasing RAM, number of servers) or adding more power to the configuration.\par
\par
\cf3 Vertical Scaling \par
Horizontal Scaling \par
\cf0\fs22 Difference Between Vertical and Horizontal Scaling in Apache\par
Vertical Scaling (or scaling up) involves increasing the capacity of a single server by adding more resources, such as CPU, RAM, or storage. This method is straightforward but has limitations since there\rquote s a maximum capacity a single server can handle1.\par
\par
Horizontal Scaling (or scaling out) involves adding more servers to your existing infrastructure to distribute the load. This method enhances redundancy and fault tolerance, as the failure of one server doesn\rquote t affect the overall system2.\par
\par
